room      lemma_6
kind      geometry
import    Newton, Principia, Andrew Motte trans., 1729 (Wikisource); Book I, Section I, Lemma VI; Plate 2, Fig. 1.
caption   As a point B on a curve approaches the point of contact A, the angle between the chord AB and the tangent AD is diminished without limit and ultimately vanishes.
final     3
ceiling   eq0 :: B \to A \;\Rightarrow\; \angle BAD \to 0

station 1
  gloss   The arc ACB rising from A, given in position, with smooth continuous curvature.
  color   arcblue  #1E6FE0
  panel
    point   A   @(0,0)    marker=dot label=$A$ at=SW
    point   ptC @(2.4,2.0) color=arcblue label=$C$ at=NW
    point   B   @(5.0,3.0) marker=dot label=$B$ at=NE
    polyline arc A ptC B  color=arcblue heart
  text
    Here is \{arcblue|the arc $ACB$}, a piece of curve fixed in position, rising from the point of contact $A$ up through $C$ to a nearby point $B$. The phrase \emph{continuous curvature} means the curve bends \emph{smoothly} — at every point, including $A$, it has one definite direction and no sharp corner. This smoothness is Newton's standing assumption, the supposition he will not let go of. \{arcblue|This arc} is the object whose ultimate behaviour near $A$ we mean to investigate.

station 2
  gloss   The chord AB cutting straight across the arc; as B slides toward A it grows ever shorter.
  color   chordgreen #00A35A
  color   arcblue    #1E6FE0
  panel
    point   A   @(0,0)    marker=dot label=$A$ at=SW
    point   ptC @(2.4,2.0) color=arcblue label=$C$ at=NW
    point   B   @(5.0,3.0) marker=dot label=$B$ at=NE
    polyline arc A ptC B  color=arcblue
    segment chordAB A B   color=chordgreen heart
  text
    Now join $A$ to $B$ by \{chordgreen|the straight chord $AB$}, a shortcut that cuts beneath \{arcblue|the arc}. Imagine the point $B$ now sliding down \{arcblue|the curve} toward $A$. As $B$ draws nearer, \{chordgreen|the chord $AB$} grows shorter and shorter, and its direction settles into the direction of the curve at $A$. It is this swivelling of \{chordgreen|the chord} that the next step compares against the tangent.

station 3
  gloss   The tangent AD grazes the curve at A; the angle BAD between chord and tangent shrinks to zero as B approaches A.
  color   tanorange  #E8770A
  color   chordgreen #00A35A
  color   anglered   #D81B60
  color   arcblue    #1E6FE0
  panel
    point   A   @(0,0)    marker=dot label=$A$ at=SW
    point   ptC @(2.4,2.0) color=arcblue label=$C$ at=NW
    point   B   @(5.0,3.0) marker=dot label=$B$ at=NE
    point   D   @(5.6,1.4) marker=dot label=$D$ at=E
    polyline arc A ptC B  color=arcblue
    segment chordAB A B   color=chordgreen
    segment tanAD A D     color=tanorange
    angle  BAD B A D      color=anglered heart label=$\angle BAD$ at=E
  text
    Draw \{tanorange|the tangent $AD$}, the straight line that just grazes \{arcblue|the curve} at $A$, matching its direction there and extending both ways. Between \{tanorange|the tangent} and \{chordgreen|the chord} opens \{anglered|the angle $BAD$}. As $B$ approaches $A$, \{chordgreen|the chord} swivels toward \{tanorange|the tangent}, and \{anglered|this angle} is diminished \emph{in infinitum}. For if \{anglered|the angle} did \emph{not} vanish, \{arcblue|the arc} would meet \{tanorange|the tangent} at a finite rectilinear angle — a sharp elbow at $A$ — and the curvature there would not be continuous, against the supposition. Hence ultimately $\angle BAD \to 0$. \textit{Q.E.D.}
